# Render coherence/adjudicate-r3's brief: the workflow's adjudicate template (prompts.mjs) with a compact work index,
# the full per-unit issue files copied into the repo for the seat to read.  Usage: python3 mkadj3.py <round-2 raw count>
import json, glob, os, subprocess, shutil, sys
SP = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
ROOT = '/home/user/maps-architecture'
R = 3
W = f'{ROOT}/docs/validation/coherence/work/round{R}-work'
os.makedirs(W, exist_ok=True); os.makedirs(f'{SP}/rr{R}', exist_ok=True)
for f in glob.glob(f'{SP}/repair-in-r{R}/*.json'): shutil.copy(f, W)
work = []
for f in sorted(glob.glob(f'{W}/*.json')):
    for i in json.load(open(f)):
        work.append({'id': i['id'], 'owner': i['owner'], 'severity': i.get('severity'), 'kind': i.get('kind'), 'reported_by': i.get('reported_by','')[:60]})
raw2 = int(sys.argv[1])
args = {'round': R, 'history': [501, 446, raw2], 'work': work}
json.dump(args, open(f'{SP}/rr{R}/adj-args.json', 'w'))
subprocess.run(['node', f'{SP}/prompts.mjs', 'adjudicate', f'{SP}/rr{R}/adj-args.json', f'{SP}/rr{R}/adjudicate-r{R}.raw.txt'], check=True)
note = f"""

ROUND-3 NOTES (orchestrator, 2026-10-01): (1) WHY THIS ADJUDICATION RUNS. Round 2's repairs filed no ruling request and the issue count fell (501 → 446 → {raw2}), so the workflow's trigger did not fire; the orchestrator runs it anyway, for routing. Round 2's verifiers found (a) fixes whose other end — a HAND-OFF a round-2 repair named for another unit — was never written, because the repairs run in parallel and each writes only its own unit; and (b) one defect reported by two to four verifiers under different ids and different owners. Round 2's adjudication (adjudication-r2.md Part H: a disposition for every issue) closed every round-1 finding; do the same for round 2's. (2) THE WORK LIST ABOVE IS AN INDEX. Each issue's full text (claim, required change, every reporter's version under `also_reported`, any `orchestrator_note`) is in docs/validation/coherence/work/round3-work/<unit>.json — read the files of every issue you rule on, whole; the verifiers' reports are docs/validation/coherence/verify-<key>-r2t.md. Identical (owner, id) pairs are merged there; different ids describing one defect are not. (3) WRITE docs/validation/coherence/adjudication-r3.md: a table of contents by unit; BINDING RULINGS (R3-01 …) where units disagree on a direction, each naming the issue ids it governs, the single correct text or decision, the unit that states it and what every other unit references (append any canon change to canon.md as a dated ruling); and PART H — for EVERY issue in the work list, under its owner's heading: FIX (the required change as written, or as a ruling amends it), MERGED into another id (named), or INVALID (closed for good, with the reason) — and, for each FIX whose change needs text in another unit, an END line under THAT unit's heading (`END of <id> (<owner>): <the exact change that unit writes>`), so every repair seat finds its whole work under its own heading. A unit with no issue of its own but with ends gets a heading and a repair seat. (4) Look first at the defects several verifiers raised — among them: a disaster look's end building by building (V06 §3.22 (3)'s `crisisLookYields`) against §8.2's and §10.2 (f)'s whole-look end; `regional_migration_pressure` → the refugee camp (item 122 (b)) against §10.2 (b)'s and DR44's \"the regional_* pressures `none`\"; the damage comparison \"above 0 and at least t\" against `level ≥ t` (§2.6, §8.6, T-V4-82 (b)); the why card's `plan` section under a disaster look (V09 against V03, V10); which calamity a disaster overlay takes (V01 §3.16 (2) against V08 §3.16 (7) (d)) and whether any dossier source reaches the `disaster` look (VPC-R2-03); and VV56-r2-01, the word `reused` in item 123 (b) against §2.8's `reused` state. That word is the program's filing text of item 123, which the owner approved (\"both and the above are yes\"), not an owner quotation: rule a reading under J122 that keeps the owner's rule whole — a ruined building's plan amended, never redrawn; restoration taking the damage away in reverse order — and never against it. (5) OWNER RULES FIRST: file g items 120–126 and their dated canon routings decide; item 125 — the dossier is the only SettlementForge data input, anything it does not give the map code supplies; item 126 (a2) — no map view on a phone at all. A verifier's required change that contradicts an owner rule is INVALID or amended to the owner's rule. adjudication-r2.md stays binding except where you supersede an entry by name. (6) Keep the file practical for the repair seats: each unit's section self-contained (rulings cited by id, every FIX and END listed with its exact change), no prose beyond what a seat needs."""
raw = open(f'{SP}/rr{R}/adjudicate-r{R}.raw.txt').read().rstrip('\n') + note
open(f'{SP}/rr{R}/adjudicate-r{R}.full.txt', 'w').write(raw)
subprocess.run(['python3', f'{SP}/finalize.py', f'coherence/adjudicate-r{R}', f'{SP}/rr{R}/adjudicate-r{R}.full.txt'], check=True)
print('work', len(work))
